.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_REG_GENERATION 3 "2026-07-13" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_reg_generation \- caller-visible identity token for a registration's fd
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

uint64_t ccol_event_loop_reg_generation(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_event_reg reg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function returns a value that only grows, and that is unique
across one loop. reg belongs to an fd, or to the bridge of a queue or
of a ccol_channel. The library mints the value one time, when it first
registers that fd, on the new-entry path of ccol_event_loop_add().
Every registration on that same fd shares the value for as long as the
fd lives. This includes a call to ccol_event_loop_modify(), because a
flip of the direction keeps the same fd and the same connection below
it. It also includes a read registration and a write registration on
the same fd. Both share one generation, because they stand for one
logical connection.

This value exists for the defensive bookkeeping of a caller across the
reuse of an fd. A caller can hold a connection object across several
asynchronous steps. It can stamp that object with the generation that
it reads right after ccol_event_loop_add() returns. It can then
compare that stamp against a fresh read. The comparison shows whether
the caller still reasons about the same logical connection. It also
shows whether something closed the fd number and an unrelated new
connection now uses it.

Basic correctness does not need the generation. The dispatch path of
ccol_event_loop already checks that a registration is live before it
calls any callback. It does that always, whether or not a caller ever
calls this function. With more than one reactor thread, the single
poller thread collects readiness, and a separate dispatch worker
thread runs the callback after that. A registration can disappear
between the collect step and the process step of one batch. Something
can remove it, or a brand new registration can reuse its fd. The loop
then safely skips it at dispatch time. It never dispatches it wrongly,
and it never causes a use-after-free. The return value of this
function is only for the bookkeeping of the application, on top of
that guarantee.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop  ccol_event_loop reg belongs to.
reg   Registration to query.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns the generation value.
Returns 0 when reg is CCOL_EVENT_REG_INVALID, or when reg fails to
resolve for another reason, such as an earlier removal. It also
returns 0 when loop is CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID, or a handle that is
stale or that something already destroyed. 0 is never a valid
generation for a real registration, because the counter starts at 1.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Safe to call at any time on any ccol_event_reg value that
ccol_event_loop_add ever returned for this loop. This covers a value
that something already removed. That removal can come from this thread
earlier, or from another thread that races this call right now. The
library resolves reg against the registration table of loop before it
dereferences anything. It therefore always finds a handle that is
stale, or that something already removed, and it reports generation 0.
It never causes a use-after-free.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call from any thread, at any time. This includes a call at the
same time as a dispatch, as ccol_event_loop_modify(), or as
ccol_event_loop_remove().
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
typedef struct conn {
    ccol_event_reg reg;
    uint64_t generation;
} conn_t;

conn_t *c = malloc(sizeof(*c));
if (!c) { perror("malloc"); exit(1); }
/* A callback of this registration can run before the next two stores.
   It gets the handle from its reg parameter, and never from c->reg. */
c->reg = ccol_event_loop_add(loop, ccol_selectable_from_fd(fd, ccol_select_read),
                        handlers, c, NULL);
c->generation = ccol_event_loop_reg_generation(loop, c->reg);

/* ... later, from another asynchronous step that holds a conn_t* ... */
if (ccol_event_loop_reg_generation(loop, c->reg) != c->generation) {
    /* stale: something closed this fd number and reused it */
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_event_loop_add (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_modify (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_remove (3)
